WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.770
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Professor Michael Gambon, who teaches

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:10.720
history at Kutztown University of Pennsylvania,
specializing in modern America. Also is author

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.651
of the forthcoming book Modern Conspiracies
in America Separating Fact from Fiction. It's

00:00:16.651 --> 00:00:20.540
out in less than two weeks. Really great to
have you on. I appreciate your time.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.119
Speaker 5: Oh, thank you for having me. I
really do. This is a great opportunity and

00:00:24.119 --> 00:00:27.289
I appreciate it.
Speaker 1: Yeah. So this this is such an important

00:00:27.289 --> 00:00:34.100
topic right now in the United States of America,
kind of in the era of COVID vaccines. Trump

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:40.210
is flat earth, the 2020 election, we could
kind of go on and on and on. We've talked

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:48.260
quite a bit about general techniques and strategies
to talk to people about beliefs that may not

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:53.730
be based, in fact. But I think we can all
kind of feel that there's a difference between

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:58.880
the belief that tax cuts for the rich are
good for the economy, kind of in one bucket.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:06.580
And on the other side, the belief that, I
don't know, there is a secret plot to create

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:12.590
the COVID pandemic in order to create an opportunity
to vaccinate people, which will then allow

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:17.570
mind control. And, you know, on and on and
on. We can feel that there's sort of some

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:22.020
difference between those two things. Can you
first talk a little bit about the difference

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:27.660
between beliefs that might just be wrong and
what we would characterize as a conspiracy

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:30.280
theory?
Speaker 5: Yeah, I think I mean, when I started

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:35.090
teaching this class, I really wanted it to
be a research class. I mean, how do you figure

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:41.520
out how to apply a basic template for logic?
So what I always tell the students, I said,

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.070
you have a claim. It has to be matched with
a proof, it has to be. And then we can talk

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:53.260
about degrees of evidence. So we just finished
up Sandy Hook and Cunanan in my class. And

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:57.160
one of the tools that you have is if you have
somebody making a claim, simply followed by

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:01.870
more claims with no proof in the following
in the wake of that. And that's a that's a

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:07.260
huge flag. So and that's what you tend to
always want. I tell me tune your ear to that.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:13.220
You know, when you hear this kind of hopscotching
ish Gallup, you know, moving along the surface

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:19.530
type of approach, if it has no depth and that's
a problem. So we can talk about tax cuts and

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:24.950
economics, fine. But if you're going to claim
that a microchip was in the, you know, COVID

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:30.480
vaccination. Show me the proof. And the other
way I look at it, if you have the big claims,

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.450
the giant global conspiracies, big claim requires
big proof. It's the same, you know, basically

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:40.660
the same idea. But it's just a bigger threshold.
Speaker 1: Is there a shortcut not to changing

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:45.940
people's minds? It will get later to the changing
of minds, but to being able to ascertain whether

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:49.780
you're speaking to someone who's open to revising
their views?

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:55.430
Speaker 5: I think so. I think you start you
know, I think I one of the things I said the

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:59.450
book is you apply the 8020 rule. I mean, you
should be starting off by listening more than

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:05.260
talking. And I think that's a skill, unfortunately,
we're losing in civil discourse. And that's

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:08.490
something I'm trying to teach in class, you
know, to sit back and listen, because teachers

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:13.990
are the worst at this. We're talkers. We have
captive audiences. So first thing, listen,

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:17.950
see where they're coming from. One of the
key things you need to know is the context.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:22.751
I mean, what are, you know, where are they?
Who where do they live? And so on. You're

00:03:22.751 --> 00:03:26.940
basically trying to build a a profile of the
person you're talking to and just simply,

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:32.380
you know, you don't want to go right to a
quick conclusion and dismiss them. So that's

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:37.480
what you're always doing in any conversation.
Speaker 1: There's the other kind of side

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:42.709
to this, and I hesitate to ever say there's
another side to things when we're really talking

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:47.300
about kind of one sided circumstances. But
sometimes you will hear the claim from some

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:53.180
who assert conspiratorial beliefs that what,
you just accept everything everybody tells

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:56.940
you. You always accept the official story.
You accept what the government says you do.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:01.490
I mean, medicine has been wrong, science has
been wrong, governments have lied, etc.. What's

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:05.530
the best way to kind of handle that idea and
approach that?

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:09.490
Speaker 5: Yeah, I think I tell them and I
I've a lot of criminal justice majors, but

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:14.580
I think in general treat this like you would
if you're on a jury. So you're have a burden

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.970
of proof. But one thing you're never going
to have is 100% certainty. So you have to

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:24.490
reach that level to say, is it 51% enough?
Do I need more? So that's how you're measuring

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:31.100
this. And also, like we were just talking
about this this week, evidence evolves. You

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:36.700
have examples where in some of these conspiracies,
you're going to have revelations it might

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:41.900
take decades or technology will change and
you'll have better ways to analyze something.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:46.830
So patience is also a virtue for both of those
things. And again, you're trying to teach

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:53.000
Americans patience. That's an ongoing enterprise.
Speaker 1: Do you address the idea of sometimes

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.980
one might assert a certain belief not because
they actually believe it, but because it signals

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:02.840
that they're part of a team. And I'll give
an example of that. You know, after the 2020

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:08.030
election, there was polling done about, do
you believe Trump won? And the numbers among

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:11.730
Republicans at the time were quite high. I
think in the immediate aftermath it was like

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:17.880
57%. And that number has waned over time.
But within that 57%, presumably there were

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:22.900
some what we might call true believers who
literally believe Trump actually won. But

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:30.419
then there are others for whom claiming it
signals support for Trump or identifies them

00:05:30.419 --> 00:05:35.920
with the team, for lack of a better term.
Where does that figure in to the studies and

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:39.880
the work that you do?
Speaker 5: I think I've used a lot of polling

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:46.080
and a lot of analysis of polling, especially
by a lot of named Baranski. You have, you

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.419
know, influencers. I mean, that's a new term,
but you've always had people who lead opinions

00:05:50.419 --> 00:05:56.370
because they're trusted. They have agency,
they're public officials. So people tend to

00:05:56.370 --> 00:06:01.530
rely on that and they can guide that or they
can abuse it. I think Joe McCarthy is a classic

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:06.260
example. But one of the things that came up
and going on, you know, of all the reading

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:11.220
I did, Wired magazine had a great story on
how coming on is like an alternate reality

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:17.000
game. So what you have is you have a universe.
You're basically it's world building, so you've

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:25.580
got certainty and rewards. So within that
group, you have that recognition. And I think

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:29.370
what that lends people in very uncertain times,
and this is very historical. I mean, you could

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:35.090
have said this in 1919 or today. It gives
people an anchor to hang on to in a very difficult,

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:40.750
fearful, uncertain time. So whether I'm a
leader or a follower, I mean, there are uses

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:46.940
for these things that, you know, can be manipulated.
Speaker 1: Yeah. That you touched on something

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:52.030
which we've we've talked about before, which
is if you take something like Sandy Hook as

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:57.040
an example or 911 maybe are good examples
of this. Sometimes it seems like one of the

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:03.530
primary motivating factors in getting people
to choose one of these assertions rather than

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:10.330
what we might call the official empirically
based version of the story, is that the randomness

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:15.960
with which bad things happen in the world
is extraordinarily unsettling. And so for

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:22.940
some folks, it's almost more pacifying to
believe. It wasn't a random thing that could

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:28.770
have happened at any time it was planned,
it was deliberate, and there's more certainty

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:34.520
in the world. Is that an important psychologically
motivating factor for more than just a few

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:38.580
of these?
Speaker 5: Absolutely. I use a book by Rob

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:44.200
Brotherton called Suspicious Minds, and he
talks about the messy desk test where people

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:48.580
instinctively want to impose order on something
that simple. But that applies to a lot of

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:55.500
things in life. So rather than be overwhelmed,
you can you make this construct and it works

00:07:55.500 --> 00:08:00.750
toward intellectual values. But also, you
know, you could say instinct. What you might

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:06.000
seem is common sense. So one of the things
we suspect is always threats and we tend to

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:10.380
associate these threats with the other. And
it could, you know, usually, you know, the

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:15.410
brand new immigrant, I mean, that's a classic
nativist go to in a lot of these conspiracies.

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:22.950
You saw that in 16 with the 3 million boats.
It's a constant drumbeat, even during COVID.

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:28.650
Who's bringing COVID while these other people
them. That's why when I first started the

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:33.519
class, I looked at the Protocols of the Elders
of Zion, and it's amazing how that keeps coming

00:08:33.519 --> 00:08:38.409
back in different forms, but it's basically
the same thing. That's about as old a story

00:08:38.409 --> 00:08:42.519
as it gets. But I saw updates to this this
year. It's amazing.

00:08:42.519 --> 00:08:47.620
Speaker 1: Yeah. Let's talk a little bit about
anti-Semitism. I, as a Jewish person myself,

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:51.600
you know, I've been aware of anti-Semitism
for as long as I can remember, being aware

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:59.249
of anything and a lot of the modern conspiracy
theories, sometimes much more subtly than

00:08:59.249 --> 00:09:04.430
others, go back to or have their roots in
in an anti-Semitic idea. You know, the idea

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:12.050
of the other bringing disease, that's a classic
anti-Semitic idea from, you know, 800, 900

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:21.929
years ago, at least globalist now often used
as a sort of catchall for wealthy Jewish man.

00:09:21.929 --> 00:09:27.160
Right. When we talk about they talk about
Soros, they talk about Klaus Schwab as a big,

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:33.869
big one here. Can you talk a little bit about
where the origins of so many of these things

00:09:33.869 --> 00:09:38.879
stemmed from anti-Semitism and whether even
some of the conspiracy theorists realize that?

00:09:38.879 --> 00:09:44.839
Speaker 5: I don't think they do, although
it's been constantly pointed out. So for Q

00:09:44.839 --> 00:09:50.350
and on, you have, you know, the endless stories
about blood drinking and child trafficking.

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:54.399
And I the first thing I automatically think
of is the blood libel, which goes back for

00:09:54.399 --> 00:09:58.800
centuries. So I think at the root of a lot
of these conspiracies, and I think Richard

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:03.611
Hofstadter pointed this out really well, you've
got a moral judgment that you're making. You're

00:10:03.611 --> 00:10:08.660
posing a moral threat, not just a political
or social or an economic one. So you've got

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:14.119
this group of identified outcasts that you're
posing as a threat to the social order. And

00:10:14.119 --> 00:10:20.079
you can go very viscerally, you know, in the
case of cure with the children. So that's

00:10:20.079 --> 00:10:25.959
a very consistent theme. The threats to children,
whether it's the Middle Ages or right now.

00:10:25.959 --> 00:10:31.209
And we can always associate that with this,
you know, labeled outcast group. It keeps

00:10:31.209 --> 00:10:37.829
reoccurring because it works. It's it's odd
to see anyone's would argue against protecting

00:10:37.829 --> 00:10:42.569
children. I mean, it's very altruistic, but
it can be very easily distorted. And it is.

00:10:42.569 --> 00:10:49.389
I saw this during the Supreme Court nomination.
Speaker 1: What do you make of flat earth

00:10:49.389 --> 00:10:57.480
surging? At a time when we have the most advanced
technology we've ever had and the most sort

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:02.240
of empirical means of ascertaining the real
shape of a planet.

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:08.079
Speaker 5: Well, we do we do chem trails in
class, so it's kind of in the same ballpark.

00:11:08.079 --> 00:11:13.110
One of the common themes I see in all these
conspiracies is there are always complexities.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:19.639
I don't know too many people who know structural
engineering enough to talk about 911 or epidemiology

00:11:19.639 --> 00:11:24.379
since 2020. So I think in all these things,
what you have is like flat earth is a way

00:11:24.379 --> 00:11:30.559
to simplify and take back an idea that experts
control. And in the meantime, what you're

00:11:30.559 --> 00:11:35.319
doing is making enormous numbers of mistakes.
But you have certainty and you don't care

00:11:35.319 --> 00:11:40.749
about the complexity. You're just basically
bypassing it. I love talking about chem trails

00:11:40.749 --> 00:11:45.019
and flat earth because my science majors lose
their minds when they look at some of the

00:11:45.019 --> 00:11:50.759
basic mistakes. But that goes back to, you
know, you know, things you can look up and

00:11:50.759 --> 00:11:57.019
assert back to the education system and, you
know, competing with the ability to oversimplify.

00:11:57.019 --> 00:11:59.980
That's that's that's kind of the tug of war
you have going on with that.

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:05.120
Speaker 1: So now let's talk about changing
minds. We've had many different conversations

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:10.339
with experts about how do you change minds?
You know, the low hanging fruit is beating

00:12:10.339 --> 00:12:16.339
people over the head with facts is rarely
effective. Showing empathy, listening a lot,

00:12:16.339 --> 00:12:22.480
as you point out, is effective. But still,
we've not really found any significant shortcut.

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:26.439
I mean, oftentimes you're talking about building
a relationship with someone, not even over

00:12:26.439 --> 00:12:32.209
a period of hours, but often over days, weeks,
months or whatever the case may be. Where

00:12:32.209 --> 00:12:35.850
what is your sense of the most effective techniques
to change minds?

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:39.779
Speaker 5: You know, well, I'm an educator,
obviously, and I've been doing this for a

00:12:39.779 --> 00:12:46.250
long time. And one of the devices I had set
up was having, you know, a group of students

00:12:46.250 --> 00:12:50.930
assert a conspiracy and a group of students
debunked a conspiracy. And we're training

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:57.600
them in basic logic facts, but also in civil
discourse. So in combination, that's where

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:01.889
I have some hope, because that's the thing
you don't have really online. You have, you

00:13:01.889 --> 00:13:07.740
know, threads where the insults break out
in seconds. And that's not the way that should

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:11.589
work. I mean, the face to face version of
this is the better way because, you know,

00:13:11.589 --> 00:13:16.540
outside the Internet, that's the way people
used to debate. You can look somebody in the

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:20.279
eye. I think it's a very hard thing to do.
I think one of the things that struck me about

00:13:20.279 --> 00:13:26.470
the recent past shows with a sense of humor,
you don't take themselves too seriously, are

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:31.809
very effective. That's what Jon Stewart proved
years ago. John Oliver, does that still I

00:13:31.809 --> 00:13:36.470
mean, you can you can say that stuff with
a smile on your face and not the dark implications

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:42.439
of disagreement. So part of its method, part
of its structure, I'm going to keep working

00:13:42.439 --> 00:13:46.170
away at it as long as I'm teaching, which
is going to be for more years than now. So

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:49.249
we'll see.
Speaker 1: When it comes to like assessing

00:13:49.249 --> 00:13:54.910
the I mean, to use the word danger, but maybe
it's not actually the right word. But when

00:13:54.910 --> 00:13:59.819
it comes to assessing the danger of widespread
belief of a particular conspiracy theory,

00:13:59.819 --> 00:14:04.689
I think many of us can understand how, at
least in a direct sense, there are some conspiracy

00:14:04.689 --> 00:14:10.199
theories, like, for example, the comet ping
pong thing that led to an actual shooting,

00:14:10.199 --> 00:14:16.609
whereas for many of us we say flat earth,
it's comparatively more benign. Some experts

00:14:16.609 --> 00:14:22.809
we've interviewed say that's true, and yet
it's rare that someone believes only one of

00:14:22.809 --> 00:14:27.529
these things and flat earth links to something
else, and it links to anti-Semitism and all

00:14:27.529 --> 00:14:32.800
these different things. So where let's try
to put together sort of a theory of this.

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:37.660
We can understand the differences between
the immediate impacts of some of these conspiracy

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:40.999
theories, but how do we evaluate them on some
kind of spectrum?

00:14:40.999 --> 00:14:46.790
Speaker 5: I think one of the things I noticed
with Q and on was that it was early on dismissed

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:52.449
as lone actors on the fringe. And I think
what obviously January 6th proved a lot of

00:14:52.449 --> 00:14:59.230
that wrong. The same systems where these beliefs
can overlap and combine, you know, with whatever

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:03.810
media platform you have, they're also the
same places that these threads can organize

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:10.040
themselves. And that's a huge concern because
you can't really shut down social media. It's

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:14.949
like playing electronic Whac-A-Mole. You can
ban somebody from a platform. They'll just

00:15:14.949 --> 00:15:20.489
moved to another one. So I I'm really concerned
after I'm sorry, after January 6th, when it

00:15:20.489 --> 00:15:26.959
comes to coordinating, acting, people will
move on their convictions. And like you mentioned

00:15:26.959 --> 00:15:32.689
in the polling, these aren't small numbers
of people. Even if you take the tiniest percentage,

00:15:32.689 --> 00:15:36.679
you still have a lot of people willing to
act on what they're doing. And that really

00:15:36.679 --> 00:15:42.509
concerns me because there's you know, the
treatment of this is almost like a crusade.

00:15:42.509 --> 00:15:46.670
So in my own classroom or, you know, I'm in
I actually am an election worker. I'll be

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:51.559
at the polls in a two weeks. I'm constantly
worried about somebody showing up, you know,

00:15:51.559 --> 00:15:55.939
to try to make sure that the election's not
stolen. I've been asked that question dozens

00:15:55.939 --> 00:16:02.239
of times in two years. So I do have concerns.
Speaker 1: Let's talk a little bit about social

00:16:02.239 --> 00:16:11.470
media in the book. Tech Napoli Neil Postman
wrote that there's a sort of naivete in thinking

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:18.970
that with any platform or technology, you
can eliminate any of the bad uses of it and

00:16:18.970 --> 00:16:25.189
only be left with the sort of good. And I'm
oversimplifying, but the idea that a platform,

00:16:25.189 --> 00:16:29.679
a technology comes with good and bad. And
the question is finding a balance of sort

00:16:29.679 --> 00:16:35.569
of regulation and education that will maximize
the good and minimize the bad. We can think

00:16:35.569 --> 00:16:40.839
about lots of great things about social media,
and also it has made the spread of conspiracy

00:16:40.839 --> 00:16:48.879
theories and false beliefs rampant like wildfire.
What should be a regulatory framework for

00:16:48.879 --> 00:16:51.989
some of these platforms?
Speaker 5: Well, that's a big that's a big

00:16:51.989 --> 00:16:58.160
question. So best example I have online is
a website called Medibank, which has moderated

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:05.630
conversations. They do have standards and
it works. But how far that could be extended

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:11.230
and spread throughout the entire Internet.
You know, that's that's the problem. And I

00:17:11.230 --> 00:17:15.300
think you pointed out, I mean, where these
things might appear in places you would have

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:20.920
never suspected. The natural parenting community
in Q and I mean, no one saw that, although

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:26.780
you could see the elements after the fact.
So it's a it's a very hard thing to talk about

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:30.890
because the more you crackdown on free speech,
the more that you're going to have the reaction.

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:36.530
And I think it becomes self-defeating after
a point. I'm back to trying to, you know,

00:17:36.530 --> 00:17:40.530
the education part before they log on. I mean,
that's that's where I'm looking at that.

